cot.output:
Detected vulnerability: CWE‐20 – Improper Input Validation

Explanation:
The qcow2_open function reads header fields directly from the qcow2 image file. In particular, when handling unknown header fields, the code does:

  if (header.header_length > sizeof(header)) {
    s->unknown_header_fields_size = header.header_length – sizeof(header);
    s->unknown_header_fields = g_malloc(s->unknown_header_fields_size);
    … (reads s->unknown_header_fields from file)
  }

No upper bound is enforced on header.header_length. A malicious qcow2 image could provide an extremely large header_length value, causing an unchecked (and potentially huge) memory allocation and read. This lack of proper validation of an externally controlled header field is a clear instance of CWE-20 (Improper Input Validation).

Result: HAS_VUL

The function does validate many of its inputs (e.g. checking that at least 5 bytes are available, that nreslevels is below a maximum, that code‐block sizes are within ranges, etc.). However, when the coding style flag JPEG2000_CSTY_PREC is set, it enters a loop:

  for (i = 0; i < c->nreslevels; i++) {
    byte = bytestream2_get_byte(&s->g);
    ... 
  }

There is no check to ensure that there are at least c->nreslevels bytes remaining in the stream before reading them. In other words, even though the initial check guarantees 5 bytes, if the PREC flag is set the function expects additional bytes (one per resolution level) but does not validate that those bytes are actually present. This omission constitutes improper input validation according to CWE-20.

Detected Vulnerability: Insufficient input validation before reading precinct size information when JPEG2000_CSTY_PREC is set.

STATUS: HAS_VUL.

history.rationale:
HAS_VUL

There are several places where input from the network is taken “at face value” without sufficient runtime boundary and sanity checks. For example, in the I1 processing code (in ikev2parent_inI1outR1), the routine retrieves the DOS (v2N) cookie payload and then computes blob.ptr and blob.len as:

  blob.ptr = dc_pbs->cur + spisize;
  blob.len = pbs_left(dc_pbs) − spisize;

without first checking that pbs_left(dc_pbs) is at least (spisize + SHA1_DIGEST_SIZE). If an attacker sends a malformed or undersized v2N payload, this could lead to an over-read. In addition, many other routines simply assume that pointers such as md and pcrc are valid and the associated payload lengths are correct, and the use of an empty passert macro means that important validity assumptions are not enforced in production. These failures to validate network input against required boundaries are classic examples of CWE-20 (Improper Input Validation).

Thus, the code does contain CWE-20 vulnerabilities.

